# SaveX

A personal budgeting app inspired by Copilot Money.

Plan a month by listing income, fixed expenses, flexible expenses and savings.
Everything you type is saved to your own Supabase account, so the plan follows
you between devices.

## Structure

- `asset/` — Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS. The whole app.
- `supabase/` — `schema.sql`, the table and row level security policies.

There is no backend service. The browser talks straight to Supabase using the
publishable key; row level security is what keeps each account to its own rows.

```
asset/src/
  app/                  routes: / (budget), /login, /settings
  components/
    AppShell.tsx        login screen vs. app, based on the session
    AuthProvider.tsx    session state from supabase.auth
    BudgetPlanner.tsx   budget UI and the debounced save pipeline
    Sidebar.tsx         nav plus the Log in / Sign out control
  lib/
    Budget.ts           sections, items, totals
    BudgetRepository.ts every read and write
    SupabaseClient.ts   the shared client
```

## Setup

1. Create a Supabase project.
2. Run `supabase/schema.sql` in the Supabase SQL editor to create `budget_items`.
3. Add `asset/.env`:

   ```bash
   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...
   ```

   Both are safe in the browser. Never put the `service_role` key here; it
   bypasses row level security.

4. Start it:

   ```bash
   cd asset && npm install && npm run dev   # http://localhost:3000
   ```

Create an account from the login screen. Signed-out visitors only ever see that
screen.

## How saving works

Typing only updates React state and flags the budget as dirty. A debounce timer
in `BudgetPlanner.tsx` waits `SAVE_DELAY_MS` (1200ms) of no typing, then writes
the whole budget in a single `upsert`. Removed rows are batched into one
`delete`. So a save costs one request, or two when items were deleted — typing
`1500` is one write, not four. The status line next to **Reset amounts** shows
where a save is up to.

## Data model

`budget_items`, one row per line item, owned by `auth.uid()`:

| column | notes |
| --- | --- |
| `id` | uuid generated in the browser, which makes upserts idempotent |
| `owner_id` | defaults to `auth.uid()`, cascade deletes with the user |
| `section` | `income` \| `fixed` \| `flexible` \| `saving` |
| `name` | free text |
| `amount` | `numeric(12,2)`, always positive |
| `position` | order within a section |

Amounts are stored as positive magnitudes. The sign lives in `BUDGET_SECTIONS`
(`income` adds, everything else subtracts), so left over is
`income − (fixed + flexible + saving)`.

## Theme

Colours are CSS variables in `asset/src/app/globals.css`, swapped by the `dark`
class on `<html>`: `#222831`, `#393E46`, `#948979`, `#DFD0B8`, with `#F8FAFC` as
the light background. Light / dark / system is selectable on the Settings page,
persists in `localStorage`, and is applied by a `beforeInteractive` script so
there is no flash of the wrong theme.

## Deploying

Vercel, with **Root Directory** set to `asset`. Add both `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`
variables before the first build — they are inlined at build time, so a missing
one fails the build and a changed one needs a redeploy. Afterwards, set the
Supabase **Authentication → URL Configuration → Site URL** to the deployed
domain so confirmation emails do not point at localhost.
